  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.6 z^2)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss, so x is the steep direction. With no noise, x swings with growing size when the learning rate is above 0.8 (2/2.5).",
    "Noise on each slope component has standard deviation 1.5·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. It is 1.5 at b=1 and exactly 0 at 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Orange arrow: the actual next step, −learning rate × noisy slope. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "Diverged: loss above 5 (its value at the bowl's steep edge) or the ball left the drawn square. The run stops there, and the ball and path are capped at the edge.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. While playing, one update happens every 4 host ticks (7.5 per second at 1×). Trails hold the start plus up to 40 steps."
  ]
}